{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MchbbBhjujePPV9xpAF5K7","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft reference gap analysis\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready. The draft is mostly well-supported. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. Three targeted retrieval needs remain.\n4\t\n5\t## Missing literatures\n6\t- Priced, swap and weighted bribery under Copeland, Borda and plurality.\n7\t- Open LLM Leaderboard (v2) documentation.\n8\t- Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML evaluation.\n9\t\n10\t## Rewrite targets\n11\t\n12\t### 1. `related-work-social-choice-bribery` (related work, high, comparator)\n13\t- **Anchor:** \"Our problem is a continuous variant: the 'voters' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores...\"\n14\t- **Why:** The paper positions itself against manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems, but the cited works are generic or about election attacks. It cites nothing on cost-priced or swap bribery, or on bribery and control under Copeland, Borda and plurality. Those are the closest comparators for the rules the planner handles heuristically.\n15\t- **Search topics:** priced and swap bribery; Copeland, Borda and plurality bribery and control; bribery under score-based voting with per-voter costs.\n16\t\n17\t### 2. `heldout-data-open-llm-leaderboard` (related work, high, benchmark_context)\n18\t- **Anchor:** \"The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks\"\n19\t- **Why:** The whole held-out evaluation uses Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots. The draft cites only the component benchmarks, not the leaderboard itself.\n20\t- **Search topics:** Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; evaluation-harness documentation.\n21\t\n22\t### 3. `intro-incentive-gaming-claim` (introduction, medium, background)\n23\t- **Anchor:** \"Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them\"\n24\t- **Why:** This is the premise of the motivating question and it has no support. One or two Goodhart or leaderboard-incentive references would be enough.\n25\t- **Search topics:** Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML benchmarks; benchmark culture and leaderboard incentives.\n26\t\n27\t## Deliberately not flagged\n28\t- Software citations (NumPy, SciPy, MILP solver). These are low value for the narrative.\n29\t- Planner heuristics such as bisection and greedy search. They are standard and described in the paper itself.\n30\t- The contamination and leaderboard-gaming paragraph, which is already densely cited.\n31\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b193e544-1bb2-487a-a796-5e20941197c3","uuid":"d9ad10d7-2304-4c8a-be55-cdb7f436ff10","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:06:12.227Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The draft is mostly well-supported. Quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not flagged. Three targeted retrieval needs remain.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Priced, swap and weighted bribery under Copeland, Borda and plurality.\n- Open LLM Leaderboard (v2) documentation.\n- Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML evaluation.\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. `related-work-social-choice-bribery` (related work, high, comparator)\n- **Anchor:** \"Our problem is a continuous variant: the 'voters' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores...\"\n- **Why:** The paper positions itself against manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems, but the cited works are generic or about election attacks. It cites nothing on cost-priced or swap bribery, or on bribery and control under Copeland, Borda and plurality. Those are the closest comparators for the rules the planner handles heuristically.\n- **Search topics:** priced and swap bribery; Copeland, Borda and plurality bribery and control; bribery under score-based voting with per-voter costs.\n\n### 2. `heldout-data-open-llm-leaderboard` (related work, high, benchmark_context)\n- **Anchor:** \"The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks\"\n- **Why:** The whole held-out evaluation uses Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots. The draft cites only the component benchmarks, not the leaderboard itself.\n- **Search topics:** Open LLM Leaderboard v2 description; evaluation-harness documentation.\n\n### 3. `intro-incentive-gaming-claim` (introduction, medium, background)\n- **Anchor:** \"Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them\"\n- **Why:** This is the premise of the motivating question and it has no support. One or two Goodhart or leaderboard-incentive references would be enough.\n- **Search topics:** Goodhart's law and metric gaming in ML benchmarks; benchmark culture and leaderboard incentives.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- Software citations (NumPy, SciPy, MILP solver). These are low value for the narrative.\n- Planner heuristics such as bisection and greedy search. They are standard and described in the paper itself.\n- The contamination and leaderboard-gaming paragraph, which is already densely cited.\n","numLines":31,"startLine":1,"totalLines":31}}}
