{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011tAcpRxgdATjrM8oQhDDfG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"status\": \"ready\",\n3\t  \"central_topic\": \"How much per-task targeted investment (score boost, measured by L1 depth and breadth over subtasks) a developer needs to make a target model rank first on public multi-task leaderboards (HELM MMLU 57 subjects, Open LLM Leaderboard 6 tasks), and how that minimal cost varies across aggregation rules.\",\n4\t  \"core_methods\": [\n5\t    \"Minimum-cost boost plan solvers per aggregation rule (exact for mean/median/maximin/plurality; verified heuristics and MILP level selection for trimmed mean/Borda/Copeland/z-score)\",\n6\t    \"Exact-replica verification and verified boundary polishing (bisection shrink, redundant-drop, pairwise transfer) of boost plans\",\n7\t    \"Breadth-constrained cost (number of tasks boosted) vs depth (L1 total boost)\",\n8\t    \"Bootstrap CIs, task-subsampling robustness, Holm/Wilcoxon tests\"\n9\t  ],\n10\t  \"comparators\": [\n11\t    \"Computational social choice: bribery, margin of victory, manipulation of voting rules\",\n12\t    \"Social-choice aggregation for multi-task/agent evaluation (Vote'n'Rank, Lanctot et al.)\",\n13\t    \"Benchmark-lottery / task-selection sensitivity\",\n14\t    \"Benchmark contamination and leaderboard gaming (Leaderboard Illusion, contamination papers)\",\n15\t    \"Robust statistics (trimmed mean, median breakdown point)\"\n16\t  ],\n17\t  \"benchmarks\": [\n18\t    \"HELM MMLU v1.13.0 (57 subjects)\",\n19\t    \"Open LLM Leaderboard contents/results (IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MuSR, MMLU-Pro)\"\n20\t  ],\n21\t  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n22\t    \"Shift from heuristic per-rule search to exact/verified per-rule solvers, then MILP level selection for rank-based rules, then verified boundary polishing; grounded only in solver design, not in model architecture changes. Modest measured solver-efficiency gains: combined score 1.0018 -> 1.0034, all runs verified correct, plan validity rate 1.0.\"\n23\t  ],\n24\t  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n25\t    \"Minimal investment depends strongly on aggregation rule (mean vs median vs min vs rank-based rules)\",\n26\t    \"Verified exact/near-exact plans with small gains from polishing over earlier heuristics\",\n27\t    \"Plausibility of targeted gains via data contamination or targeted training\",\n28\t    \"Uncertainty and robustness of conclusions to task subsets and snapshots\"\n29\t  ],\n30\t  \"queries\": [\n31\t    {\n32\t      \"query\": \"bribery and margin of victory in voting rules computational complexity\",\n33\t      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n34\t      \"rationale\": \"Minimal targeted boost to make a target rank first is a cost-minimizing manipulation/bribery problem; margin of victory and bribery are the closest formal analogues for the cost-vs-rule comparison (depth L1 cost, breadth cost).\"\n35\t    },\n36\t    {\n37\t      \"query\": \"manipulation of Borda and Copeland rules complexity\",\n38\t      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n39\t      \"rationale\": \"Borda, Copeland and plurality are the rank-based rules solved by MILP level selection; their manipulation/bribery hardness motivates exact vs heuristic solvers.\"\n40\t    },\n41\t    {\n42\t      \"query\": \"rank aggregation of multi-task benchmark scores with social choice rules (Borda, Copeland, maximin)\",\n43\t      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n44\t      \"rationale\": \"Comparator literature for the aggregation rules studied (mean, median, trimmed mean, min, Borda, plurality, Copeland); beyond starter items 5 and 11, includes Vote'n'Rank-style benchmark aggregation.\"\n45\t    },\n46\t    {\n47\t      \"query\": \"robustness of rankings to task selection and aggregation choice in multi-task benchmarks\",\n48\t      \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n49\t      \"rationale\": \"Reviewers expect connection to benchmark-lottery/task-sensitivity work: how rule choice changes who is first, which is the baseline for the paper's rule-dependence claim.\"\n50\t    },\n51\t    {\n52\t      \"query\": \"trimmed mean median robust location estimator breakdown point\",\n53\t      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n54\t      \"rationale\": \"Mean, median and trimmed mean differ in how many per-task boosts are needed; breakdown point/influence function theory explains the predicted cost ordering.\"\n55\t    },\n56\t    {\n57\t      \"query\": \"benchmark contamination training on test data inflates language model leaderboard scores\",\n58\t      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n59\t      \"rationale\": \"Targeted investment is modeled as including subset-related data in training; contamination/leakage literature supports the plausibility and magnitude of per-task score gains.\"\n60\t    },\n61\t    {\n62\t      \"query\": \"adaptive overfitting and holdout reuse in machine learning leaderboards\",\n63\t      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n64\t      \"rationale\": \"Strategic targeting of leaderboard components is a form of overfitting to the evaluation; Ladder/holdout-reuse work frames leaderboard gaming and limits of the claims.\"\n65\t    },\n66\t    {\n67\t      \"query\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard IFEval GPQA MuSR MMLU-Pro BBH MATH benchmark suite\",\n68\t      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n69\t      \"rationale\": \"Scientific context for the 6-task Open LLM Leaderboard data used; the task components need citations.\"\n70\t    },\n71\t    {\n72\t      \"query\": \"HELM MMLU 57 subjects per-subject scores language model evaluation\",\n73\t      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n74\t      \"rationale\": \"57-subject HELM MMLU v1.13.0 is the many-task setting; the dataset and evaluation framework need accurate citations.\"\n75\t    },\n76\t    {\n77\t      \"query\": \"confidence intervals and uncertainty for model rankings on leaderboards bootstrap\",\n78\t      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n79\t      \"rationale\": \"The run reports bootstrap CIs and subsample-task robustness over rankings; ranking-uncertainty literature supports the methodology.\"\n80\t    }\n81\t  ],\n82\t  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n83\t    \"Any query naming or paraphrasing the withheld published paper on this exact research question (title, authors, results), or pages citing/discussing it\",\n84\t    \"Generic: benchmarking, optimization, scientific method, paper writing\",\n85\t    \"Chronology/search-history phrasing (cycles, attempts, incumbent scores)\",\n86\t    \"Model-architecture queries (transformer, scaling) — no architectures are trained\",\n87\t    \"Starter-literature titles already supplied (MMLU, BBH, HELM, Benchmark Lottery, Lanctot, Leaderboard Illusion, Zhou, Yang, lm-eval-harness, Handbook of COMSOC) except to resolve BibTeX\"\n88\t  ],\n89\t  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n90\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b193e544-1bb2-487a-a796-5e20941197c3","uuid":"830fb21d-204d-42c4-81f6-645ffddc2629","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:06:15.983Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/reference_query_plan.json","content":"{\n  \"status\": \"ready\",\n  \"central_topic\": \"How much per-task targeted investment (score boost, measured by L1 depth and breadth over subtasks) a developer needs to make a target model rank first on public multi-task leaderboards (HELM MMLU 57 subjects, Open LLM Leaderboard 6 tasks), and how that minimal cost varies across aggregation rules.\",\n  \"core_methods\": [\n    \"Minimum-cost boost plan solvers per aggregation rule (exact for mean/median/maximin/plurality; verified heuristics and MILP level selection for trimmed mean/Borda/Copeland/z-score)\",\n    \"Exact-replica verification and verified boundary polishing (bisection shrink, redundant-drop, pairwise transfer) of boost plans\",\n    \"Breadth-constrained cost (number of tasks boosted) vs depth (L1 total boost)\",\n    \"Bootstrap CIs, task-subsampling robustness, Holm/Wilcoxon tests\"\n  ],\n  \"comparators\": [\n    \"Computational social choice: bribery, margin of victory, manipulation of voting rules\",\n    \"Social-choice aggregation for multi-task/agent evaluation (Vote'n'Rank, Lanctot et al.)\",\n    \"Benchmark-lottery / task-selection sensitivity\",\n    \"Benchmark contamination and leaderboard gaming (Leaderboard Illusion, contamination papers)\",\n    \"Robust statistics (trimmed mean, median breakdown point)\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n    \"HELM MMLU v1.13.0 (57 subjects)\",\n    \"Open LLM Leaderboard contents/results (IFEval, BBH, MATH Lvl 5, GPQA, MuSR, MMLU-Pro)\"\n  ],\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n    \"Shift from heuristic per-rule search to exact/verified per-rule solvers, then MILP level selection for rank-based rules, then verified boundary polishing; grounded only in solver design, not in model architecture changes. Modest measured solver-efficiency gains: combined score 1.0018 -> 1.0034, all runs verified correct, plan validity rate 1.0.\"\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n    \"Minimal investment depends strongly on aggregation rule (mean vs median vs min vs rank-based rules)\",\n    \"Verified exact/near-exact plans with small gains from polishing over earlier heuristics\",\n    \"Plausibility of targeted gains via data contamination or targeted training\",\n    \"Uncertainty and robustness of conclusions to task subsets and snapshots\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n    {\n      \"query\": \"bribery and margin of victory in voting rules computational complexity\",\n      \"category\": \"core_method\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Minimal targeted boost to make a target rank first is a cost-minimizing manipulation/bribery problem; margin of victory and bribery are the closest formal analogues for the cost-vs-rule comparison (depth L1 cost, breadth cost).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"manipulation of Borda and Copeland rules complexity\",\n      \"category\": \"c