d limits of the claims.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"Open LLM Leaderboard IFEval GPQA MuSR MMLU-Pro BBH MATH benchmark suite\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"Scientific context for the 6-task Open LLM Leaderboard data used; the task components need citations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"HELM MMLU 57 subjects per-subject scores language model evaluation\",\n      \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n      \"rationale\": \"57-subject HELM MMLU v1.13.0 is the many-task setting; the dataset and evaluation framework need accurate citations.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"query\": \"confidence intervals and uncertainty for model rankings on leaderboards bootstrap\",\n      \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The run reports bootstrap CIs and subsample-task robustness over rankings; ranking-uncertainty literature supports the methodology.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n    \"Any query naming or paraphrasing the withheld published paper on this exact research question (title, authors, results), or pages citing/discussing it\",\n    \"Generic: benchmarking, optimization, scientific method, paper writing\",\n    \"Chronology/search-history phrasing (cycles, attempts, incumbent scores)\",\n    \"Model-architecture queries (transformer, scaling) — no architectures are trained\",\n    \"Starter-literature titles already supplied (MMLU, BBH, HELM, Benchmark Lottery, Lanctot, Leaderboard Illusion, Zhou, Yang, lm-eval-harness, Handbook of COMSOC) except to resolve BibTeX\"\n  ],\n  \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}","numLines":90,"startLine":1,"totalLines":90}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvshZkyA9vxbcao5BjSS","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DfPXfTHr6aBnz4JFXAJot7","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"grep -n -i -E \"goodhart|Open LLM Leaderboard|Rankings influence|continuous variant|Copeland\" paper.tex | head -30"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8373,"cache_read_input_tokens":16291,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8373},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b193e544-1bb2-487a-a796-5e20941197c3","uuid":"1ca140a7-6836-4ddd-9f66-00113f81f325","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:06:16.353Z","request_id":"req_011CgvshZWLgyr2mBWvmYNWx"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DfPXfTHr6aBnz4JFXAJot7","type":"tool_result","content":"38:Multi-task leaderboards turn a matrix of per-task scores into a single ranking through an aggregation rule, and a developer who can raise a target model's score on selected tasks, for example by training on related data, may be able to push it to first place. How much such targeted investment is needed, and how that quantity depends on the aggregation rule, is the question that motivates this work. We formalize targeted investment as non-negative, independent per-task score gains, priced either by total points (L1) or by the number of tasks touched (L0), with success meaning the target is strictly first under eight rules (mean, median, trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda, plurality, Copeland, maximin). We then study a verification-first planner that returns the cheapest plan it can certify, combining exact solvers for four rules with verified heuristics for the others. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, scored by a trusted evaluator against its own reference solver, the planner reaches a mean efficiency of 1.0032 relative to the reference, against 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every response well-formed; the evaluation is qualified. Because per-rule costs were not retained, we do \\emph{not} test whether required investment varies across rules, and the results say nothing about real training effects.\n42:Multi-task benchmarks are the standard instrument for comparing machine-learning models: a leaderboard collects scores of many models on many sub-datasets and an aggregation rule, most often the arithmetic mean, converts each model's score vector into a rank \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them. One class of actions is to include data related to some of the sub-datasets in a model's training, which raises its scores on those tasks. Prior work documents that leaderboards are sensitive to task selection \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, to aggregation choices \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}, to private testing and uneven sampling \\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and to benchmark leakage \\citep{Zhou2023DontMY,Sainz2023NLPEI,Yang2023RethinkingBA}. What remains less examined is a quantitative, rule-by-rule account of how large a targeted investment must be for a specific model to reach first place.\n51:\\item A verification-first planner (exact solvers for mean, median, maximin and plurality; verified heuristics for trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda and Copeland; plan verification, pruning, boundary polishing and a safe fallback) whose design is documented at the level of the evaluated source (Section~\\ref{sec:planner}).\n52:\\item A held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards against a reference solver, naive baselines and two controls, with a separate development comparison of three planner configurations on HELM MMLU (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n64:\\paragraph{Multi-task benchmarks and their aggregation.} Broad suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench and its hard subset, HELM and SuperGLUE summarize models by averaging over many sub-tasks \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Srivastava2022BeyondTI,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollowingEF,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Hendrycks2021MeasuringMP,Rein2023GPQAAG,Sprague2023MuSRTT,Wang2024MMLUProAM}. Critiques show that the mean is a modeling choice with consequences: task choice can reorder methods \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, leaderboard design embeds assumptions about user utility \\citep{Ethayarajh2020UtilityII}, and aggregate statistics need uncertainty-aware treatment \\citep{Agarwal2021DeepRL}. Social-choice formulations replace the mean with voting rules for models, agents or systems \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}. We use that rule family not to recommend a rule but as the set of aggregators over which a targeted-investment cost is defined.\n68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,Faliszewski2006HowHI,Xia2012ComputingTM,Conitzer2016BarriersTM}. Complexity results depend on the rule, on the number of candidates, and on tie-breaking \\citep{Obraztsova2011TiesMC,Yang2014ElectionAW,Dey2014KernelizationCO}. Our problem is a continuous variant: the ``voters'' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores (not preferences directly), and cost is measured in score points or in touched tasks. We borrow the strict-win convention, under which ties count against the target.\n85:Table~\\ref{tab:rules} lists the eight rules, with the planner's solution method from its source and the reference solver's method from the evaluator source. Mean, median, trimmed mean (dropping the lowest and highest 20\\% of tasks), maximin (worst-task score) and z-score mean (per-task standardization recomputed on the modified matrix) act on score levels; Borda (mean per-task rank), plurality (number of tasks on which a model is strictly first) and Copeland (pairwise task-majority wins) act on per-task orderings and are piecewise constant in $g$. Raising the target's own scores can only help it under all rules except z-score mean, because that rule re-standardizes each column using the modified matrix and so can move rivals' values in either direction; the planner's source treats it as non-monotone.\n103:Copeland & orderings & level-move ratio greedy (L1); greedy (L0) & level-table family and greedy\\\\\n116:\\item[H3 (MILP selection).] A MILP level-selection layer lowers cost for the discrete rules (Borda, Copeland, plurality) and the trimmed mean relative to the base solvers.\n124:\\item \\textbf{Per-rule candidate plans.} Exact solvers are used for mean, median, maximin and plurality; verified heuristics (floor-family plans, convex-hull and ratio greedy, restarted greedy for L0) for trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda and Copeland (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}). A uniform-gain plan (L1) or headroom-ordered fill (L0) is always added as a candidate.\n136:\\paragraph{Data and splits.} Development uses the 57 HELM MMLU v1.13.0 subject columns as tasks. The held-out evaluation uses two boards built from the Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots: a six-benchmark board from the \\texttt{contents} table and a sub-task-level board from the \\texttt{results} files for the same model pool, each restricted to the top $\\min(N,200)$ models. The evaluator verifies snapshot checksums and checks that the held-out boards are disjoint from the development data. Development seeds are 11 and 12; held-out seeds are 9001 and 9002.\n138:\\paragraph{Compared configurations.} The held-out study evaluates one frozen planner: base solvers plus boundary polishing. For a development-only component comparison, three configurations were scored on HELM MMLU: the base solvers (Base); the base solvers with an additional MILP level-selection layer for Borda, Copeland, plurality and trimmed mean and an exchange polish for z-score L0 (Base+MILP); and the base solvers with boundary polishing (Base+Polish). The Base+Polish source does not contain the MILP layer, so these are alternative extensions of the base rather than a cumulative chain. Only Base+Polish was carried into the held-out study. No development-plane baseline metric was retained.\n146:\\caption{Held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards (two evaluator seeds). (a) Main efficiency of the verified planner and of the better of two naive baselines per case, against the reference level of 1. (b) Main, control and combined efficiency of the planner on a zoomed axis; dots are the two per-seed combined values.}\n219:We formalized targeted investment on multi-task leaderboards and evaluated a verification-first planner that returns only plans re-checked against an exact replica of the aggregation rule. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, the planner's mean efficiency relative to an evaluator-owned reference solver was 1.0032, against 0.8166 for the better naive baseline, with a plan validity rate of 1.0 and a qualified outcome. A development comparison on HELM MMLU suggests that boundary polishing improves cost slightly (combined 1.00339 versus 1.00183 for the base solvers) at higher compute, whereas an additional MILP level-selection layer (with a z-score exchange polish) changed nothing measurable. These conclusions are conditional on the declared investment model, rest on two evaluator seeds, and concern planner cost-efficiency. The central question that motivated the study, how much required investment varies with the aggregation rule, needs per-rule, per-target cost tables with paired uncertainty and the shuffled-null comparison; these remain future work, as do models with spillover and heterogeneous task costs.\n236:\\paragraph{Instance definition.} An instance is a tuple $(X,i,r,\\text{cost model})$ with $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ restricted to the field, a target row $i$, a rule $r$ from Table~\\ref{tab:rules}, and L1 or L0 costs. Per-task headroom is $h_t=c_t-x_{it}$. Rule definitions follow the evaluator's rule engine: Borda scores are minus the mean per-task rank (ties share half credit), plurality counts tasks on which a model is the unique maximizer, Copeland counts pairwise task-majority wins with half credit for ties, and the trimmed mean drops $\\lfloor T/5\\rfloor$ tasks from each end. The main cases are the 32 combinations of rule, target rank (2, 10) and cost measure per board; control cases use rank 2 and L1 for each of the 8 rules, once for the permuted input and once for the shuffled null.\n248:\\caption{Held-out metrics from the sealed final evaluation (Open LLM Leaderboard boards).}\n287:\\paragraph{Data.} Development: HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 (summary date 2025-01-10), 57 subject files, exact-match column, each file pinned by SHA-256 in the task manifest. Held-out: Open LLM Leaderboard \\texttt{contents} at revision \\texttt{9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2} (parquet SHA-256 \\texttt{f13a7061\\ldots dca5}) and \\texttt{results} at revision \\texttt{aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5} (derived sub-task file SHA-256 \\texttt{c4536f9f\\ldots 4a7f}). Flagged models and rows with missing values are excluded, and a newer submission replaces an older duplicate. Licenses: no dataset-level license is declared for the score data; the HELM code is Apache-2.0; \\texttt{contents} carries per-model Hub license fields. Use is limited to research analysis of published scores.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b193e544-1bb2-487a-a796-5e20941197c3","uuid":"45aff23d-023f-40d4-9b06-9e519663442a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T09:06:16.386Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"38:Multi-task leaderboards turn a matrix of per-task scores into a single ranking through an aggregation rule, and a developer who can raise a target model's score on selected tasks, for example by training on related data, may be able to push it to first place. How much such targeted investment is needed, and how that quantity depends on the aggregation rule, is the question that motivates this work. We formalize targeted investment as non-negative, independent per-task score gains, priced either by total points (L1) or by the number of tasks touched (L0), with success meaning the target is strictly first under eight rules (mean, median, trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda, plurality, Copeland, maximin). We then study a verification-first planner that returns the cheapest plan it can certify, combining exact solvers for four rules with verified heuristics for the others. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, scored by a trusted evaluator against its own reference solver, the planner reaches a mean efficiency of 1.0032 relative to the reference, against 0.8166 for the better of two naive baselines, with every response well-formed; the evaluation is qualified. Because per-rule costs were not retained, we do \\emph{not} test whether required investment varies across rules, and the results say nothing about real training effects.\n42:Multi-task benchmarks are the standard instrument for comparing machine-learning models: a leaderboard collects scores of many models on many sub-datasets and an aggregation rule, most often the arithmetic mean, converts each model's score vector into a rank \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. Rankings influence adoption, publication and funding, so developers have an incentive to act on them. One class of actions is to include data related to some of the sub-datasets in a model's training, which raises its scores on those tasks. Prior work documents that leaderboards are sensitive to task selection \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, to aggregation choices \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}, to private testing and uneven sampling \\citep{Singh2025TheLI}, and to benchmark leakage \\citep{Zhou2023DontMY,Sainz2023NLPEI,Yang2023RethinkingBA}. What remains less examined is a quantitative, rule-by-rule account of how large a targeted investment must be for a specific model to reach first place.\n51:\\item A verification-first planner (exact solvers for mean, median, maximin and plurality; verified heuristics for trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda and Copeland; plan verification, pruning, boundary polishing and a safe fallback) whose design is documented at the level of the evaluated source (Section~\\ref{sec:planner}).\n52:\\item A held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards against a reference solver, naive baselines and two controls, with a separate development comparison of three planner configurations on HELM MMLU (Section~\\ref{sec:results}).\n64:\\paragraph{Multi-task benchmarks and their aggregation.} Broad suites such as MMLU, BIG-bench and its hard subset, HELM and SuperGLUE summarize models by averaging over many sub-tasks \\citep{Hendrycks2020MeasuringMM,Srivastava2022BeyondTI,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Liang2022HolisticEO,Wang2019SuperGLUEAS}. The Open LLM Leaderboard aggregates six benchmarks \\citep{Zhou2023InstructionFollowingEF,Suzgun2022ChallengingBT,Hendrycks2021MeasuringMP,Rein2023GPQAAG,Sprague2023MuSRTT,Wang2024MMLUProAM}. Critiques show that the mean is a modeling choice with consequences: task choice can reorder methods \\citep{Dehghani2021TheBL}, leaderboard design embeds assumptions about user utility \\citep{Ethayarajh2020UtilityII}, and aggregate statistics need uncertainty-aware treatment \\citep{Agarwal2021DeepRL}. Social-choice formulations replace the mean with voting rules for models, agents or systems \\citep{Colombo2022WhatAT,Rofin2022VotenRankRO,Lanctot2023EvaluatingAU}. We use that rule family not to recommend a rule but as the set of aggregators over which a targeted-investment cost is defined.\n68:\\paragraph{Computational social choice.} Manipulation, bribery and margin-of-victory problems ask how little must change for a chosen candidate to win under a voting rule \\citep{Bartholdi1989TheCD,Faliszewski2006HowHI,Xia2012ComputingTM,Conitzer2016BarriersTM}. Complexity results depend on the rule, on the number of candidates, and on tie-breaking \\citep{Obraztsova2011TiesMC,Yang2014ElectionAW,Dey2014KernelizationCO}. Our problem is a continuous variant: the ``voters'' are tasks, the intervention changes one candidate's scores (not preferences directly), and cost is measured in score points or in touched tasks. We borrow the strict-win convention, under which ties count against the target.\n85:Table~\\ref{tab:rules} lists the eight rules, with the planner's solution method from its source and the reference solver's method from the evaluator source. Mean, median, trimmed mean (dropping the lowest and highest 20\\% of tasks), maximin (worst-task score) and z-score mean (per-task standardization recomputed on the modified matrix) act on score levels; Borda (mean per-task rank), plurality (number of tasks on which a model is strictly first) and Copeland (pairwise task-majority wins) act on per-task orderings and are piecewise constant in $g$. Raising the target's own scores can only help it under all rules except z-score mean, because that rule re-standardizes each column using the modified matrix and so can move rivals' values in either direction; the planner's source treats it as non-monotone.\n103:Copeland & orderings & level-move ratio greedy (L1); greedy (L0) & level-table family and greedy\\\\\n116:\\item[H3 (MILP selection).] A MILP level-selection layer lowers cost for the discrete rules (Borda, Copeland, plurality) and the trimmed mean relative to the base solvers.\n124:\\item \\textbf{Per-rule candidate plans.} Exact solvers are used for mean, median, maximin and plurality; verified heuristics (floor-family plans, convex-hull and ratio greedy, restarted greedy for L0) for trimmed mean, z-score mean, Borda and Copeland (Table~\\ref{tab:rules}). A uniform-gain plan (L1) or headroom-ordered fill (L0) is always added as a candidate.\n136:\\paragraph{Data and splits.} Development uses the 57 HELM MMLU v1.13.0 subject columns as tasks. The held-out evaluation uses two boards built from the Open LLM Leaderboard snapshots: a six-benchmark board from the \\texttt{contents} table and a sub-task-level board from the \\texttt{results} files for the same model pool, each restricted to the top $\\min(N,200)$ models. The evaluator verifies snapshot checksums and checks that the held-out boards are disjoint from the development data. Development seeds are 11 and 12; held-out seeds are 9001 and 9002.\n138:\\paragraph{Compared configurations.} The held-out study evaluates one frozen planner: base solvers plus boundary polishing. For a development-only component comparison, three configurations were scored on HELM MMLU: the base solvers (Base); the base solvers with an additional MILP level-selection layer for Borda, Copeland, plurality and trimmed mean and an exchange polish for z-score L0 (Base+MILP); and the base solvers with boundary polishing (Base+Polish). The Base+Polish source does not contain the MILP layer, so these are alternative extensions of the base rather than a cumulative chain. Only Base+Polish was carried into the held-out study. No development-plane baseline metric was retained.\n146:\\caption{Held-out evaluation on two Open LLM Leaderboard boards (two evaluator seeds). (a) Main efficiency of the verified planner and of the better of two naive baselines per case, against the reference level of 1. (b) Main, control and combined efficiency of the planner on a zoomed axis; dots are the two per-seed combined values.}\n219:We formalized targeted investment on multi-task leaderboards and evaluated a verification-first planner that returns only plans re-checked against an exact replica of the aggregation rule. On two held-out Open LLM Leaderboard boards, the planner's mean efficiency relative to an evaluator-owned reference solver was 1.0032, against 0.8166 for the better naive baseline, with a plan validity rate of 1.0 and a qualified outcome. A development comparison on HELM MMLU suggests that boundary polishing improves cost slightly (combined 1.00339 versus 1.00183 for the base solvers) at higher compute, whereas an additional MILP level-selection layer (with a z-score exchange polish) changed nothing measurable. These conclusions are conditional on the declared investment model, rest on two evaluator seeds, and concern planner cost-efficiency. The central question that motivated the study, how much required investment varies with the aggregation rule, needs per-rule, per-target cost tables with paired uncertainty and the shuffled-null comparison; these remain future work, as do models with spillover and heterogeneous task costs.\n236:\\paragraph{Instance definition.} An instance is a tuple $(X,i,r,\\text{cost model})$ with $X\\in[0,100]^{N\\times T}$ restricted to the field, a target row $i$, a rule $r$ from Table~\\ref{tab:rules}, and L1 or L0 costs. Per-task headroom is $h_t=c_t-x_{it}$. Rule definitions follow the evaluator's rule engine: Borda scores are minus the mean per-task rank (ties share half credit), plurality counts tasks on which a model is the unique maximizer, Copeland counts pairwise task-majority wins with half credit for ties, and the trimmed mean drops $\\lfloor T/5\\rfloor$ tasks from each end. The main cases are the 32 combinations of rule, target rank (2, 10) and cost measure per board; control cases use rank 2 and L1 for each of the 8 rules, once for the permuted input and once for the shuffled null.\n248:\\caption{Held-out metrics from the sealed final evaluation (Open LLM Leaderboard boards).}\n287:\\paragraph{Data.} Development: HELM MMLU release v1.13.0 (summary date 2025-01-10), 57 subject files, exact-match column, each file pinned by SHA-256 in the task manifest. Held-out: Open LLM Leaderboard \\texttt{contents} at revision \\texttt{9c09a7cae43334062a82cb164f2ef255013dafa2} (parquet SHA-256 \\texttt{f13a7061\\ldots dca5}) and \\texttt{results} at revision \\texttt{aa81ecc38fdc5708254b833923368970efdf5ef5} (derived sub-task file SHA-256 \\texttt{c4536f9f\\ldots 4a7f}). Flagged models and rows with missing values are excluded, and a newer submission replaces an older duplicate. Licenses: no dataset-level license is declared for the score data; the HELM code is Apache-2.0; \\texttt{contents} carries per-model Hub license fields. Use is limited to research analysis of published scores.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
